\subsection{Similarity}{

\code{similarity} says how similar an option is to the target as a function of
their distance on the wheel. The model assumes the similarity depends only
on the distance, the same for every target and on both sides of it. It is
1 at the target and 0 at the option opposite it. There are three choices:
\itemize{
\item A numeric vector holds fixed values, one per distance in option steps,
in the order 0, 1, ..., \code{n_options / 2}: the first value belongs to the
target itself and must be 1, the last to the option opposite the target.
Schurgin et al. (2020) measured them in a separate perceptual task and
scaled them to range from 0 to 1. With 360 options in 1-degree steps,
\code{similarity[k + 1]} is the similarity at \code{k} degrees.
\item \code{"laplace"} estimates a similarity that falls exponentially with the
distance \code{x} in radians, \code{exp(-x / lambda)}, and adds the parameter
\code{lambda}, its width in radians.
\item \code{"vonmises"} estimates a similarity of the shape \code{exp(-kappa * (1 -   cos(x)))} and adds the parameter \code{kappa}, its concentration.
}

The two shapes are the Laplace and von Mises activation functions of
Oberauer (2021, eq. 2); his Laplace rate is \code{1 / lambda}. Both are scaled
to run from 1 at the target to 0 opposite it, like a measured similarity.
Scaling changes the value of \code{d} that describes the data, not the shape.
The distance is measured on the response circle: with 180 options for
orientation, \code{x = pi} is the option opposite the target, 90 degrees of
orientation away.

\code{lambda} and \code{kappa} are modelled on the log scale and take formulas like
\code{d}, including continuous predictors and random effects. Their default
priors are \code{normal(-0.5, 1)} on \code{log(lambda)}, centred at 0.61 radians (35
degrees), and \code{normal(1.5, 1)} on \code{log(kappa)}, centred at 4.5. The
formula never chooses the similarity: naming \code{lambda} in it requires
\code{similarity = "laplace"}, and \code{kappa} requires \code{"vonmises"}. A value fixed
in the formula, such as \code{lambda = -1}, is on the log scale.

Oberauer, K. (2021). Measurement models for visual working memory: A
factorial model comparison. Psychological Review.
https://doi.org/10.1037/rev0000328
}

\subsection{Noise}{

\code{noise} sets the distribution of the noise on each option's signal.
\code{"gaussian"}, the default, is the model of Schurgin et al. (2020), with
normal noise of unit variance. Its response probabilities have no closed
form, so the likelihood reads them from a table (see the grid limits
below). With \code{"gumbel"} the noise follows the standard Gumbel (type I
extreme value) distribution. The largest of independent Gumbel variables
is again Gumbel distributed, which turns the probability of choosing an
option into the softmax of \code{d * f(x)} over all options:
\verb{exp(d * f(x)) / sum(exp(d * f(x')))} (Oberauer, 2021, eq. 4). This needs
no table, so there are no grid limits on \code{d}, \code{lambda} or \code{kappa}, and
\code{d_max} is not used.

Oberauer (2021) crossed three choices: the activation function (von
Mises or Laplace), the response-selection rule (Luce's choice rule, or
signal detection with Gumbel or with normal noise) and whether the
precision \code{kappa} is the same in all conditions. \code{tcc()} fits the cells
with a signal-detection rule: \code{noise} selects the noise, \code{similarity}
the activation function, and the formula for \code{kappa} or \code{lambda} holds it
constant (\code{kappa ~ 1}) or lets it vary with the conditions. The von Mises
similarity with Gumbel noise is the cell that fit best in his comparison
and that he named the signal discrimination model, which \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}} fits on
a continuous response scale. On a wheel of \code{n_options} options,
\code{tcc(similarity = "vonmises", noise = "gumbel")} gives the \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}} density
at each option's centre times the option width \code{2 * pi / n_options}, with
the same \code{kappa} and \code{c = d * sqrt(2 * pi / kappa) / (1 - exp(-2 * kappa))} in the default parametrization of \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}}.

Oberauer found the fits with Gumbel and with normal noise always very
close (p. 5). Gumbel noise needs no table, so it skips the build before
sampling and has no grid limits, and it is the noise to compare with
\code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}}. Per gradient it is not cheaper: on \link{oberauer_lin_2017}, with \code{d}
and \code{kappa} per subject and set size, it cost 1.4 times the normal noise
with a von Mises similarity (about as much as \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}}) and 1.5 times with
a fixed similarity; with a continuous predictor of \code{kappa}, which makes
every trial its own cell, it cost 6.2 times (174 against 28 ms). Keep
normal noise to stay with the model of Schurgin et al. and to compare \code{d}
with their d'. The two \code{d} are not on one scale.
The standard Gumbel distribution has an SD of \code{pi / sqrt(6)} = 1.28, but
matching the response probabilities takes a larger factor. On the
probabilities of the normal-noise model, the Gumbel \code{d} with the smallest
Kullback-Leibler divergence was 2.6 to 2.9 times the normal \code{d} for
values of the latter from 0.5 to 2, and 1.9 to 2.4 times at 8, for the
similarities of the examples below.
}

\subsection{Response}{

With \code{units = "radians"}, \code{resp_error} is the response minus the target
in radians, as for \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}} and \code{\link[=mixture2p]{mixture2p()}}, and each value is assigned to
the nearest of the \code{n_options} options (+-pi fall into the same option).
With \code{units = "bins"}, it is the chosen option's offset from the target in
option steps. The model treats the response as a choice among
\code{n_options} discrete options, so \code{n_options} should be the number of
options the participant could choose from.
}

\subsection{Grid limit on d}{

\code{d} is modelled on the log scale, so it is positive, effects on it are
multiplicative, and priors on its coefficients are priors on \code{log(d)}.
Keep those priors proper: as \code{d} approaches 0 the likelihood levels off
at that of guessing uniformly, so under a flat prior on \code{log(d)} the
posterior cannot be normalised, and a cell with little information
drifts towards \code{d = 0} without end. The default prior is proper. This
holds for either noise; the rest of this section and the next apply to
normal noise only. The likelihood reads the response probabilities from a table on a grid
of \code{d} values up to \code{d_max} in steps of 0.02, built once per chain
by quadrature and interpolated with a cubic spline (error below 1e-6 in
log probability). Beyond \code{d_max} the table is extended
linearly, with the log probability of each response allowed to fall but
not to rise. This keeps the sampler moving during warmup but is not the
model: past \code{d_max} the likelihood no longer favours a larger \code{d}, so a
posterior that reaches the end of the grid follows the prior beyond it,
with estimates that mean nothing. \code{bmm()} warns after the fit when any
posterior draw of \code{d} passes \code{d_max}; then refit with a larger \code{d_max}.
The default of 30 lies far above the values of \code{d} from 0.6 to 2.0 that
\code{tcc()} estimates for set sizes 1 to 8 in \link{oberauer_lin_2017} with the
similarity of the examples below (\code{d} depends on the similarity). Each
extra unit adds 50 table rows, which costs build time and memory but
nothing per gradient. A large \code{d} is weakly identified
when the similarity is broad, because the probability of reporting the
target then grows slowly with \code{d}.
}

\subsection{Grid limits with an estimated similarity}{

With \code{similarity = "laplace"} or \code{"vonmises"} the table has two
dimensions: \code{d} in steps of 0.1 up to \code{d_max}, and \code{log(lambda)} or
\code{log(kappa)} in steps of 0.1. \code{lambda} runs from 0.004 to 21.7 radians
(0.23 to 1245 degrees) and \code{kappa} from 0.02 to 53,500, which reaches
from a similarity so sharp that no option but the target resembles it,
to one that falls almost as a straight line (Laplace) or a raised cosine
(von Mises) with distance. Between the grid nodes the interpolated log
probability errs by at most 2e-4 on responses with a probability above
1e-4 up to \code{d = 12} and by 6e-4 up to \code{d = 30}; on any response, by
1.5e-3 up to \code{d = 12} and 1e-2 up to \code{d = 30}.
Beyond either end of either axis the table is extended linearly, and
above \code{d_max}, as with a fixed similarity, the log probability of a
response may fall with \code{d} but never rise. When no response lies far
from the target, a large \code{d} with a broad similarity describes the data
almost as well as a small \code{d} with a sharp one, and along that ridge a
rising extension would let \code{d} run off without bound. \code{bmm()} warns after
the fit when any posterior draw of \code{d} passes \code{d_max}, or of \code{lambda} or
\code{kappa} leaves its grid. There is no argument to widen the grid of
\code{lambda} or \code{kappa}: use a prior that keeps the posterior inside.

Each chain builds this table once before sampling. With the default
\code{d_max = 30} that took 10 s (Laplace) and 18 s (von Mises) per chain on
the machine bmm is developed on, and each chain process held 61 MB and
91 MB.
}

\subsection{Posterior predictive checks}{

\code{pp_check(fit)} compares observed and predicted offsets in option steps.
\code{pp_check(fit, resp_var = "error")} shows the same on the scale of
\code{resp_error}, which for radians is the view \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}} and \code{\link[=mixture2p]{mixture2p()}} fits
give; add \code{type = "bars_binned"} and name a condition column in \code{group}
for histograms per condition. \code{resp_var = "abs_error"} shows the distance from the target,
the quantity the likelihood depends on. See \code{\link[=pp_check.bmmfit]{pp_check.bmmfit()}}.
\code{posterior_predict()} and \code{predict()} return offsets in option steps
whatever \code{units} is; multiply by \code{2 * pi / n_options} for radians.
}

\subsection{Comparing with continuous models}{

The log-likelihood of \code{tcc()} is a log probability mass over options,
whereas \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}} and \code{\link[=mixture2p]{mixture2p()}} return a log density in radians. The two
differ by the log of the option width, \code{log(2 * pi / n_options)} per
observation, so summed over \code{N} observations by \code{N * log(2 * pi / n_options)}; for 360 options, \code{log(2 * pi / 360)} is -4.048. Before
comparing LOO estimates, convert the TCC log-likelihood into a density:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{ll <- brms::log_lik(fit_tcc) + log(n_options / (2 * pi))
loo_tcc <- loo::loo(ll, r_eff = loo::relative_eff(exp(ll), chain_id =
  rep(seq_len(brms::nchains(fit_tcc)), each = brms::ndraws(fit_tcc) /
  brms::nchains(fit_tcc))))
loo::loo_compare(loo_tcc, brms::loo(fit_sdm))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{loo_compare()} warns that the models do not share the same y variable,
because \code{tcc()} stores the binned response; the warning is expected here.
The comparison is fair only if the responses were themselves recorded on
\code{n_options} discrete options, as on a 360-degree colour wheel.
}

\subsection{Speed}{

The fit is fastest when rows that share a value of \code{d} are adjacent, so
bmm offers to sort the data by the predictors of \code{d}, as for \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}}.
Continuous predictors of \code{d} are supported at the per-trial cost.

With \code{lambda} or \code{kappa} estimated, rows are grouped by their values of
both parameters. On \link{oberauer_lin_2017} (15,200 trials in 152 cells of
subject and set size) one gradient took 2.4 ms with both parameters
predicted by set size and subject, and 28 ms with a continuous predictor
of \code{lambda} as well, which makes every trial its own cell. Estimating
the similarity also about doubles the number of gradients per iteration.
}
}
